    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-888.s8">
        <fig id="article-888.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Horizontal Section of the Human Eye. This diagram depicts a horizontal section of the human eye. The anterior segment includes the cornea, anterior chamber, iris, and posterior chamber. The lens is suspended by the ciliary body and zonular fibers. The primary ocular cavity contains the vitreous body and the hyaloid canal. The outer layers comprise the conjunctiva, sclera, choroid, and retina, with the fovea centralis positioned at the posterior pole. The lateral aspects display the rectus lateralis and rectus medialis muscles. Posterior structures include the optic nerve, optic nerve sheath, and the central retinal artery (A. centralis retinae). The sulcus circularis corneae is indicated near the anterior segment. Henry Vandyke Carter, Public Domain, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray869" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-888.s9">
        <fig id="article-888.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Histology of the Retina. Hematoxylin and eosin&#x02013;stained section&#x000a0;displays layers from the internal limiting membrane to the photoreceptor outer segments (cyan arrow), interfacing with the retinal pigment epithelium. Author: commons wikimedia wiki User:Librepath License: https://creativecommons.org/share-your-work/licensing-considerations/compatible-licenses Blue arrow added</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Retina_--_intermed_mag" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-888.s10">
        <fig id="article-888.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Fluorescent Micrograph of Retinal Photoreceptor Architecture. Immunofluorescent staining highlights photoreceptor outer segments in green and neuronal cell bodies and processes in red, illustrating the vertical organization of retinal layers. Image courtesy of Dr. Robert Fariss, National Eye Institute, NIH Licence: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="24912842829_67804ffd8f_b" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-888.s11">
        <fig id="article-888.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Cellular Architecture of the Human Retina. This illustration shows the various layers and cell types of the retina, starting from the Bruch membrane (BM) and the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), which contains pigment granules (P). The photoreceptor layer includes the outer segments (OS) and inner segments (IS) of the rod cells (R) and cone cells (C), with the external limiting membrane (indicated by the triangle symbol) marked by a dotted line. The cell bodies are located in the outer nuclear layer (ONL), while synaptic connections occur in the outer plexiform layer (OPL) and inner plexiform layer (IPL). The inner nuclear layer (INL) contains horizontal cells (H), bipolar cells (Bi), M&#x000fc;ller cells (M), and amacrine cells (A). At the base, the ganglion cell layer (GC) contains the ganglion cells (G) and their axons (Ax) that form the optic nerve. Hartpete, CC BY-SA 3.0 &#x0003c;http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/&#x0003e;, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Retinal__Layers__xCreative__Commonsx" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
